<DocsLayout title="Marketplace Templates — Kuberfy Documentation" description="Deploy pre-configured open-source apps from the Kuberfy marketplace in one click.">
  <h1>Marketplace Templates</h1>

  <p>
    The <strong>Marketplace</strong> lists 36 pre-configured open-source applications — databases, monitoring tools, self-hosted alternatives to common SaaS products — that
    deploy as a Docker image with environment variables and volumes already set up. It's a third way to create an application, alongside a Git repository or a manual image.
  </p>

  <div class="callout-info">
    <p class="mb-0">
      <strong>Source:</strong> Template definitions (image, port, environment variables, volumes) are pulled from Dokploy's and Coolify's public template repositories,
      filtered down to apps that run as a single container — Kuberfy doesn't support multi-container Compose stacks yet. The result is committed to
      <code>apps/api/src/data/marketplace-templates.json</code>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Deploying a Template</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Open a project and go to <strong>Marketplace</strong>.
    </li>
    <li>Search or filter by tag, then select a template.</li>
    <li>
      Review the generated configuration: application name, image, internal port, and environment variables. Values marked as secrets (API keys, database passwords) are
      generated automatically — you can reveal or edit them before deploying.
    </li>
    <li>
      Click <strong>Deploy</strong>. Kuberfy creates the application, attaches any required volumes, and starts a normal image-based deployment — the same rolling update
      described in <a href="/docs/deployments">Deploying Applications</a>.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>What Gets Created</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Application:</strong> A standard image-based application (<code>buildType: "image"</code>), editable afterward like any other.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Environment variables:</strong> Pre-filled from the template. Values flagged <code>secret: true</code> (passwords, tokens) get a random value generated in the
      browser instead of a placeholder.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Volumes:</strong> Any paths the template declares (e.g. a database's data directory) are created and mounted automatically.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Updating the Template List</h2>
  <p>
    Templates are synced from upstream with <code>bun run scripts/fetch-marketplace-templates.ts</code>, which rewrites <code>marketplace-templates.json</code> after checking
    each candidate resolves to a single container. Run it and commit the result to pick up new or updated templates.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
